this became the new normal in Hong Kong  in 2019 almost daily violence between  masked protesters and police the spark  that eventually set the streets and  emotions on fire was a decision to  ignore the outcry of millions of people  and push through a controversial  extradition bill which would have  allowed any suspect in the city to be  tried in the courts of mainland China  and the people just went nuts people  thought if we don't fight now this is  our last fight and if we don't fight now  we'll have no chance and we're fighting  quarry Mo was one of the legislators who  marched with hundreds of thousands to  protest against the bill Hong Kong's  chief executives said the bill was  needed to extradite a Hong Kong murder  suspect from Taiwan but people feared it  not only put them at the mercy of  China's Communist Party controlled legal  system but would also allow Beijing to  seize anyone who set foot in the city  the unrest continued into July and then  the protesters raced the stakes  aljazeera was one of the first  international networks to broadcast  massed young people storming the Hong  Kong government headquarters in an  attempt to disrupt any more meetings by  the legislature the bill is dead and  finally two months later chief executive  Carrie Lam conceded and withdrew the  bill but the unrest continued as the  movement went on the focus changed to  police brutality and that kind of like  making people think that Hong Kong is  becoming more like a authoritarian  regime and that brings more people out  as a result Kelvin Lam was voted into  office in historic district council  elections in November  nearly half of Hong Kong's 7 million  people went to the polls giving the  city's pro-democracy camp and opposition  parties control of a political body for  the first time Hong Kong will not be the  same again because the anger or the  discontent of the other side he will  still be kind of embedded in people's  minds it has been mostly quiet since its  of the year there are still sporadic  protests but instead of thousands on the  streets it's now hundreds or dozens and  police are much quicker to use crowd  control measures and make arrests but  many fear this could just be the calm  before the storm there is so much hatred  between the protesters and the  experiment Hong Kong can never be really  government again the protesters here say  the government hasn't made any attempt  to address their concerns  instead it's taking a tougher stance  against dissent making people fear that  it's just a matter of time before scenes  like this once again take over the  streets the big apollon al Jazeera Hong  Kong
 

